+# Enforcement policy bundle
+
+The control plane serves this directory to every PDP (OPA) as a signed bundle at
+`GET /enforcement/bundles/complens.tar.gz`. It must contain exactly two files:
+
+| File        | Purpose |
+|-------------|---------|
+| `main.rego` | The Envoy `ext_authz` entrypoint — `package envoy.authz`. Shadow-first: unconfigured hosts always return `allowed=true` and only record the would-be verdict. |
+| `data.json` | Per-system configuration pushed with the policy: `{"systems": {"<host>": {"mode": "shadow"\|"enforce", "fail": "open"\|"closed", "policy_id": "...", "allowed_roles": [...]}}}` |
+
+The bundle revision is `sha256(main.rego \0 data.json)[:12]`, so every entry in
+the decision log is linked to the exact bundle that produced it. Editing either
+file changes the revision and every PDP picks it up on its next poll.
+
+`systems` starts empty: a host you have not configured is never blocked. Add
+entries here, or flip an existing one with
+`POST /enforcement/systems/{host}/mode` (admin key required), which rewrites
+`data.json` in place.
+
+Point `ENFORCEMENT_POLICY_DIR` at this directory if you relocate it. Do not
+confuse it with `POLICY_DIR`, which holds the YAML policy-as-code definitions in
+`policies/` that the detective engine evaluates.
