MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

33 workflow files lived in .github/workflows. One (ci.yml) verified the code.
The other 32 were one-shot workflow_dispatch jobs, each carrying a base64
tarball of source, each holding 'contents: write', each unpacking itself and
committing the result — 464 KB of opaque build-your-own-repo machinery whose
inputs no reviewer can read. They are how this codebase was assembled and they
explain most of its inconsistencies: duplicate import aliases suffixed to avoid
collisions, ORM and Pydantic classes interleaved in models.py in append order,
and two registrations of the same route. Removed, along with reorganize.sh and
the stray tests/add-tests.yml.

README-comp-lens-clean.md was a near-duplicate README that still said 'Add your
preferred license here' next to an MIT LICENSE file. Deleted.

Documentation now matches the code: the clone URL pointed at a repository name
that does not exist (Comp-lens-2.0), the API table documented /ai/systems and
/reports.csv where the app serves /ai-systems and /reports/csv, and FIXES.md
referenced a policies/compliance.rego that was never there. README gains a
section on the two policy directories, which are genuinely easy to confuse —
policies/ (detective, YAML, POLICY_DIR) versus enforcement_policy/ (preventive,
Rego, ENFORCEMENT_POLICY_DIR) — and DEPLOY.md documents the API key the
installer now generates.

