@@ -0,0 +1,185 @@
+# Comp-Lens — maturity audit
+
+**Repository:** `shalak97/Comp-lens` at `7bde48a`
+**Date:** 14 August 2026
+**Method:** static review of all 17.9k lines of Python, plus execution — full test suite, ruff, `alembic upgrade head` into an empty database with an autogenerate diff against the ORM models, coverage run, and a reproduction of the production boot path.
+
+## Summary
+
+The codebase is in better shape than its history suggests. 299 tests pass, ruff is clean, secrets are handled properly, the auth model is tenant-scoped, and several production guards (SQLite refused in production, demo connector gated, evidence signing key required) are genuinely well built and well commented. That is a real foundation.
+
+The problems are not in the business logic. They are at the seams — where the code meets its own deployment, its own database schema, and its own documentation. Three of the four highest-severity findings would only bite on the first real install, which is exactly why the test suite never saw them.
+
+The root cause is visible in the repository itself. `.github/workflows/` held 33 files. One ran CI. The other 32 were one-shot jobs, each carrying a base64-encoded tarball of source code, each holding `contents: write`, each unpacking itself and committing the result. 464 KB of build-your-own-repository machinery whose inputs no reviewer can read. Almost every inconsistency below is a fingerprint of that process: imports aliased with `_evm` suffixes to avoid collisions between generated blocks, ORM and Pydantic classes interleaved in append order, the same route registered twice by two different generators, a whole second FastAPI application left behind after its endpoints were partially copied elsewhere.
+
+**What changed:** everything in "Fixed" is implemented on branch `maturity/consistency-pass` — 6 commits, 62 files, +1,245 / −2,148. The suite is now 310 passing tests (11 new, covering the two most dangerous findings). Nothing in "Not fixed" was touched; it is scoped and prioritised below.
+
+---
+
+## Fixed
+
+### 1 · The one-command installer produced a stack that could not boot — Critical
+
+`install.sh` wrote `APP_ENV=production` alongside a blank `COMP_LENS_API_KEYS`, with the comment *"Leave blank for no auth (fine behind a VPN)"*. But `app/main.py`'s lifespan handler fails closed:
+
+```
+RuntimeError: Refusing to start: APP_ENV=production but no API keys are
+configured. Set COMP_LENS_API_KEYS to enable authentication.
+```
+
+Reproduced directly with the installer's exact `.env` values. The container would crash-loop, the installer would spin for its full 180-second timeout, and the user would land on *"App did not report healthy within 3 minutes."* The two halves of the codebase disagreed about the same variable, and each half was individually defensible — the guard is correct, the installer was not.
+
+The installer now mints an admin key, writes it as `<key>:*`, prints it with a ready-to-paste `curl`, and fails fast with a clear message if an existing `.env` has `APP_ENV=production` and no key. `docker-compose.yml` promotes the variable to required (`:?`) so the failure surfaces at `up` rather than in a health-check timeout.
+
+Two smaller contradictions in the same path: the installer advertised `/docs`, which is disabled in production by design (`expose_api_docs` defaults false) — it now explains how to enable it, and `EXPOSE_API_DOCS` is plumbed through compose. `.env.example` carried the identical blank-key trap.
+
+### 2 · Two handlers on one route, with incompatible contracts — High
+
+```
+app/main.py:722   @app.post("/v1/integrate/ai-systems/{system_id}/to-risk")   tenant from JSON body
+app/main.py:1704  @app.post("/v1/integrate/ai-systems/{system_id}/to-risk")   tenant from query param
+```
+
+FastAPI matches the first registration. The second — the documented one, and the one consistent with every other `/v1/integrate/*` route — was unreachable. A caller passing `?tenant_id=acme` was silently scoped to `"default"`.
+
+In a compliance product, silently writing a risk-register entry to the wrong tenant is not a routing nit. Kept the query-parameter handler and taught it to accept the body form too, so no existing caller breaks. A regression test now asserts route uniqueness across the app.
+
+### 3 · Migrations had drifted 68 differences from the models — High
+
+A database built the way production builds one — `alembic upgrade head` on an empty file — did not match `app/models.py`. `compare_metadata` reported 68 pending differences:
+
+- **Three declared indexes were never created**: `ix_evidence_documents_tenant_id`, `ix_evidence_concept_hits_tenant_id`, `ix_evidence_concept_hits_doc_id`. Every tenant-scoped evidence-document query was a full table scan. This is the finding most likely to be felt as "the product got slow" rather than "the product broke".
+- **65 columns declared `NOT NULL` in the models were nullable in the database.** The schema accepted rows the application assumes cannot exist.
+
+Nothing caught it because the test suite builds its schema with `Base.metadata.create_all()` and never executes a migration. The two code paths that produce a schema had never been compared.
+
+The new revision creates the indexes and tightens the constraints. Columns carrying a server default are backfilled from it first, so the migration is safe on a populated database; columns without one are deliberately left to fail loudly, because a NULL identifier or timestamp is real missing data that needs a decision rather than an invented value. `render_as_batch` makes the `ALTER`s work on SQLite and is a no-op on PostgreSQL, so one file covers both. Verified: upgrade → 0 diffs, downgrade → re-upgrade → 0 diffs.
+
+`tests/test_migrations.py` now runs the real migration chain and asserts autogenerate finds nothing left to do, so a model change without a migration fails CI instead of production. It also guards the three indexes by name and asserts the revision graph has exactly one head.
+
+### 4 · A second, dead FastAPI application holding live endpoints — High
+
+`app/app.py` was a 198-line FastAPI app. Nothing imported it, no Dockerfile or compose file ran it, coverage reported 0% — and it held the only implementations of two endpoints the enforcement design depends on: the OPA bundle at `GET /enforcement/bundles/complens.tar.gz` and the decision-log sink at `POST /enforcement/logs`. It also duplicated `bundle_revision()`, `status()`, `systems()`, `decisions()` and `set_mode()` from `app/services/enforcement.py` — two implementations free to disagree — and its bundle builder called `read_bytes()` unguarded, so an unprovisioned policy directory produced a 500 traceback.
+
+Underneath sat a quieter problem: `POLICY_DIR` defaulted to `app/policy/`, which contains no `main.rego` and no `data.json`. The enforcement feature was inert on every deployment — `bundle_revision()` returned `"unversioned"`, `_systems_config()` returned `{}`, and the dashboard's Enforcement view showed an empty fleet forever. The actual `main.rego` sat unreferenced in the repository root.
+
+Both endpoints now live in `app.main` on top of `app.services.enforcement`, and they require an API key (the standalone app had none). `BundleNotProvisioned` turns a missing bundle into a 503 naming the files it wants. `main.rego` moved to `enforcement_policy/` with a real `data.json` and a README; the Dockerfile copies it. Resolution order is `ENFORCEMENT_POLICY_DIR` then the legacy `COMPLENS_POLICY_DIR`, so the variable is no longer confusable with `POLICY_DIR`, which points at the entirely unrelated YAML policy definitions.
+
+8 new tests cover bundle contents, revision stability under policy change, gzip and plain decision logs, malformed-entry tolerance, and the 503 path.
+
+### 5 · Endpoints importing modules that have never existed — Medium
+
+```python
+try:
+    from app.middleware_core import normalize as _norm
+    from app.services.middleware_service import MiddlewareService as _MW
+    ...
+except Exception:
+    persisted = {"note": "middleware not available; events returned but not stored"}
+```
+
+Neither module exists anywhere in the repository's history. `/v1/documents/ingest` and `/v1/documents/upload` always took the fallback branch — and because the `except` was `Exception` rather than `ImportError`, it would equally have swallowed a genuine database failure and reported the same reassuring note. Replaced with an explicit helper that states plainly what happened, and dropped the unused DB session dependency from both handlers.
+
+### 6 · Deployment manifests contradicting the code's own security defaults — Medium
+
+`render.yaml` is the only deployment manifest in the repo. It set `ENABLE_DEMO_CONNECTOR=true` in production. `app/config.py` documents the opposite intent in a comment: *"Defaults OFF in production so nobody can fabricate 'pass' evidence against a real compliance database."* The manifest was overriding the guarantee on the one target the repo ships. Set to false.
+
+It also never set `TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS`, though `config.py` says *"set to 1 behind Render."* Left at 0, the rate limiter keys anonymous callers on Render's proxy IP — every anonymous request shares one bucket, so a single noisy client throttles everyone. Set to 1.
+
+### 7 · Configuration and packaging sprawl — Medium
+
+`conftest.py` says *"the `-p no:randomly` default already lives in pyproject.toml."* There was no `pyproject.toml`. The flag had to be passed by hand in CI, and the project had no package metadata at all, which is why both CI and every developer needed a `PYTHONPATH` export to `import app`.
+
+Added `pyproject.toml` as the single tool-configuration file — metadata, pytest settings, and the ruff rules from the now-deleted `ruff.toml`. `pythonpath` and `alembic.ini`'s `prepend_sys_path` replace the `PYTHONPATH=$GITHUB_WORKSPACE` export. Test ordering is pinned in `addopts` rather than depending on every caller remembering the flag — several modules share a database file, so random order would be flaky.
+
+`COMP_LENS_SECRET_KEY` was set in CI and one test, and read by nothing. Removed. The version was a `"1.2.0"` literal in `main.py` while the README and ruff config called the project "2.0"; `app.__version__` is now the single source.
+
+### 8 · Documentation describing a different repository — Low
+
+The clone URL in both `README.md` and `DEPLOY.md` pointed at `Comp-lens-2.0.git`, a repository that does not exist. The API table documented `/ai/systems` and `/reports.csv`; the app serves `/ai-systems` and `/reports/csv`. `FIXES.md` referenced a `policies/compliance.rego` that was never there. `README-comp-lens-clean.md` was a near-duplicate README still saying *"Add your preferred license here"* next to an MIT `LICENSE` file — deleted.
+
+README gains a section on the two policy directories, which are genuinely easy to confuse and were the direct cause of finding 4.
+
+### 9 · Test artifacts landing in the repository root — Low
+
+`tests/test_aigov_merkle_policy_forecast.py` writes evidence to `./test_v16_ev`. `.gitignore` covered `test_evidence*/` but not this. Every suite run left 344 KB of untracked `.jsonl` files one `git add -A` away from being committed. Added, along with `.coverage` and the tool cache directories.
+
+### 10 · Container build hardening — Low
+
+The Dockerfile installed `gcc` and `libpq-dev` into the runtime image. Every dependency ships a manylinux wheel (`psycopg[binary]` bundles libpq; `cryptography` and `paramiko` are prebuilt) — verified by a wheel-only install. They contributed nothing but image size and CVE surface. Removed, with `--only-binary=:all:` so a missing wheel fails the build loudly rather than falling back to a compile that cannot succeed.
+
+CI gains a container-build job asserting the dashboard HTML, framework JSON data, enforcement bundle and YAML policies are actually present in the image. `.dockerignore` excludes `*.html` and `*.json`; those patterns are root-anchored so nested assets do ship today, but nothing was checking, and the file now says so explicitly.
+
+---
+
+## Not fixed — recommended next
+
+Ordered by value per unit of risk. None of these are breakage; they are the difference between code that works and code a team can safely change.
+
+### A · Two engines answer "how trustworthy is this control?" — highest priority
+
+`app/services/trust_telemetry.py` (296 lines, 5 lanes, drift signal, `unified_trust`) and `app/grc_platforms/trust_telemetry.py` (223 lines, `TrustPolicy`, freshness multiplier, banding, `GRCTrustTelemetry`) are independent scoring implementations reached through different endpoints — `/trust/risk-telemetry` and `/v1/grc-trust/score`. They will disagree, and there is no test asserting they agree.
+
+For a product whose output is an audit artifact, two authoritative answers to the same question is the most serious *conceptual* inconsistency in the codebase — more consequential than anything fixed above, and the one thing an auditor would actually catch. The same pattern repeats more mildly across `services/crosswalk.py`, `grc_platforms/crosswalk.py` and `frameworks.py`.
+
+**Do:** decide which model is correct, make the other a thin adapter over it, and add a test pinning the two endpoints to the same number for a fixed fixture.
+
+### B · `app/main.py` is 1,886 lines and 151 routes
+
+Sixty-six function-local `from app...` imports, seven `response_model=` declarations across 151 routes. Deferred imports are a legitimate technique for breaking cycles; at this density they are a symptom — each generator appended its handlers and imported locally to avoid touching the header.
+
+**Do:** split into `APIRouter` modules by domain (`evidence`, `connectors`, `audits`, `grc`, `enforcement`, `policy`, `threat`) with `main.py` reduced to app construction and `include_router` calls. Low risk, entirely mechanical, and it makes every subsequent change reviewable. Add `response_model=` as you go — the OpenAPI schema is currently almost untyped, which undercuts the `/docs` the product advertises.
+
+### C · The API surface is versioned two different ways
+
+32 routes under `/v1`, 119 unversioned. `/policy` and `/policies` and `/v1/policy` all exist. `/grc/risks` sits beside `/tprm/vendors` and `/v1/grc-sync` and `/v1/grc-trust`.
+
+**Do:** pick one convention. Mount everything under `/v1` and keep the unversioned paths as permanent redirects — cheap to do while the routers are being split (B), expensive later once integrators depend on both spellings.
+
+### D · Model definitions scattered across seven modules
+
+`app/models.py` alone interleaves 18 SQLAlchemy tables with 13 Pydantic schemas in the order generators appended them; six more `*_models.py` modules sit alongside it.
+
+**Do:** `app/models/` for ORM, `app/schemas/` for Pydantic, one module per domain. Mechanical, and it makes the ORM/Pydantic boundary — which is where validation bugs hide — visible again.
+
+### E · 77 broad `except Exception` handlers
+
+Twelve in `main.py`. Finding 5 is exactly what this costs: a permanent failure was indistinguishable from a transient one for the lifetime of the feature.
+
+**Do:** narrow to the exception actually expected and log the rest. Prioritise `main.py` and the connectors, where a swallowed error means silently missing evidence — the failure mode a compliance tool can least afford.
+
+### F · Single-instance ceiling, undocumented
+
+The rate limiter, the enforcement decision store and the scheduler are all in-process. Each is honestly commented, but nothing states the combined consequence: **running two replicas silently breaks all three** — limits multiply by replica count, decisions scatter, schedules double-fire. `POST /enforcement/systems/{host}/mode` also rewrites `data.json` inside the container, so a shadow→enforce flip is lost on the next deploy.
+
+**Do:** state the ceiling in `DEPLOY.md` now; move the three stores to PostgreSQL or Redis before anyone scales out. Related: `render.yaml` puts the evidence store on `/tmp`, which is ephemeral on Render — the inline comment says so, but the manifest ships that way.
+
+### G · Coverage is 73%, concentrated in the wrong places
+
+Lowest: `services/attestation.py` 17%, `services/resolver.py` 20%, `services/dependency_graph.py` 25%, `services/crawler.py` 26%, `connectors/secondary.py` 28%, `connectors/aws.py` 31%. The connectors are the trust boundary — they turn third-party API responses into compliance evidence.
+
+**Do:** raise connector coverage first, with recorded fixtures for malformed and partial upstream responses. Then add a coverage floor to CI so it ratchets rather than drifts.
+
+### H · Smaller items
+
+- `app/services/evidence_sign.py`'s docstring promises *"a deterministic per-deploy key"*; the code falls back to the hardcoded constant `"comp-lens-unconfigured-signing-key"`. Not exploitable — production refuses it — but the docstring overstates the guarantee. Its `except Exception: k = None` around the config import also leaves `prod = False`, so a config failure would sign with the world-known key.
+- `app/config.py` builds `settings` at import time and `app/database.py` builds the engine at import time. This is why `conftest.py` has to set environment variables before any import and why its own `fresh_db` fixture carries a caveat about modules that bound the engine at collection. A `get_settings()` FastAPI dependency plus a lazily-created engine would make configuration testable.
+- `app/static/dashboard.html` is a 224 KB single file with no build step or tests.
+- `cli/complens_policy.py` was referenced by nothing; it now has a `complens-policy` console entry point in `pyproject.toml`, but no test.
+
+---
+
+## Verification
+
+| Check | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ruff check .` | clean | clean |
+| `pytest` | 299 passed, 1 xfailed | **310 passed, 1 xfailed** |
+| Migration ↔ model drift | **68 differences** | **0** |
+| Production boot with installer defaults | **RuntimeError, crash-loop** | **boots; `/health/live` 200** |
+| Duplicate route registrations | **1** | **0** |
+| `PYTHONPATH` required for pytest / alembic | yes | no |
+| Workflow files with `contents: write` | **32** | **0** |
+| Dead application modules | `app/app.py` (0% covered) | none |
+
+Downgrade → re-upgrade round-trip on the new migration verified clean. Wheel-only dependency install verified. The container build job is committed but unexecuted here — no Docker daemon was available in the audit environment; it will run on the first push.
-- 
