MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The first pass covered the seams: deployment, schema, documentation. This one
asks whether the platform's output is true, and finds seven issues, five of
which share one shape — an operation reporting success it did not achieve.

Most serious: 7 of 18 built-in controls report an affirmative FAIL when handed
empty telemetry, so a revoked IAM permission is recorded as 'Encryption at rest
is NOT enabled' and exported to an auditor. Eleven evaluators in the same file
correctly return not_applicable, which is what makes this an oversight rather
than a fail-closed choice.

Also: record_hash is an unkeyed SHA-256 while the code claims an attacker would
have to forge it (demonstrated: flip a verdict, recompute, integrity still
reports intact); EVIDENCE_SIGNING_KEY is required at boot but unused on that
path; documents are never signed though the migration, columns and verifier all
exist; AuditService omits the tenant check in 2 of 10 methods, leaking a control
count across tenants; /policies/import returns imported:true and discards the
policy; one failing schedule rolls back the run bookkeeping for all of them; and
the documented /findings pagination cap was never applied to its ten siblings.

Every finding has a runnable reproduction under proofs/. The evaluator sandbox,
the SSRF defence, the Merkle construction and route-level authorization were all
probed and held — recorded in the audit and in proofs/p4_evaluator.py so that
ground is not re-covered.

No production code changed in this commit.

